The Mercedes-Benz Vito 115 CDI is primarily associated with the W639 generation (2003-2014), including the facelifted models from 2010-2014. It is powered by a 2.1-litre 4-cylinder turbo-diesel engine producing 110 kW (150 hp) and 330 Nm of torque. It was available with both manual (6-speed) and automatic (5-speed) transmissions and rear-wheel drive, with some 4x4 variants also existing. It was offered in various body styles including panel van, crew cab, and passenger versions (Traveliner/Combi), with seating for up to 9 people in some configurations.

Acceleration from 0-100 km/h (0-62 mph) is around 12.2 to 14.9 seconds, depending on the specific variant and transmission. Top speed is around 179-181 km/h (111-112 mph). Fuel consumption varies, with reported averages around 8.1-8.7 L/100km (around 27-35 mpg).

The W639 Vito has a wheelbase of 3200mm or 3430mm and lengths ranging from 4748mm to 5223mm. Cargo volume ranges from 4.65 to 6.5 cubic metres. Turning radius is around 11.8 to 12.5 meters (38.7 to 41 feet). Ground clearance is listed as 140mm or 146mm (around 5.5 to 5.7 inches).

In terms of safety, the W639 Vito could achieve a 5-star ANCAP safety rating if optioned with side head-protecting airbags. It came standard with ABS and ESP. ADAS features were limited compared to modern vehicles, though some later models or optional extras included things like PARKTRONIC (parking sensors) and potentially early forms of driver assistance.

Interior features were functional, with options like air conditioning, power windows, and cruise control available. Infotainment was basic by today's standards, typically featuring a standard radio, though some aftermarket upgrades are available.

Reliability is generally considered good for the engines, though some common issues like injector seals, glow plugs, and transmission problems (specifically the 722.6 automatic gearbox) have been reported. Suspension components can also wear out. Insurance groups can vary, but some sources indicate Group A for certain models, though Sport models could be more expensive to insure. Used prices vary significantly based on age, mileage, and condition.

